{
  "dom_id": "step-e14609",
  "name": "Example: Solving Linear Congruences With Many Solutions",
  "problem": "Find the sum modulo $20$ of the solutions to the congruence $8x \\equiv 4 \\:(\\text{mod}\\:20).$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*",
  "worked_solution": "In the given congruence, we have $a=8$ and $n=20,$ so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(8,20)=4.\n$$\n\nSince $b=4$ is divisible by $4,$ our congruence has $4$ solutions.\n\nFirst, we reduce the congruence using the division property of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x & ≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ 4\\cdot 2x & ≡4\\cdot 1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4\\cdot 5) \\\\ \\cancel{4}\\cdot 2x & ≡\\cancel{4}\\cdot 1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{4}\\cdot 5) \\\\ 2x & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in the new congruence, we have $a_1=2$ and $n_1=5,$ so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a_1,n_1)=\\text{gcd}(2,5)=1.\n$$\n\nThis means our congruence has a unique solution modulo $5.$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $2\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:5).$ To find the inverse of $2\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:5),$ we compute the exponents of $2\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:5)$ until we get a result of $1 \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:5)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2} & ≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ {2}^{3} & ≡4\\cdot 2≡8≡1\\cdot 5+3≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ {2}^{4} & ≡3\\cdot 2≡6≡1\\cdot 5+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $2 \\cdot 3 \\equiv 1 \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:5),$ which means that $3$ is the multiplicative inverse of $2$ (modulo $5$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $3,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 3\\cdot 2x & ≡3\\cdot 1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ (3\\cdot 2)x & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ x & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get one solution, $x \\equiv 3 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:5).$ \n\nBut our original congruence was modulo $20$ (not modulo $5$). To find all the solutions modulo $20,$ we repeatedly add $5$ modulo $20$ until we reach the number $3$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ {x}_{2} & ≡3+5≡8 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ {x}_{3} & ≡8+5≡13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ {x}_{4} & ≡13+5≡18 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ {x}_{5} & ≡18+5≡23≡1\\cdot 20+3≡3≡{x}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x\\equiv 3, 8, 13, 18 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:20).$ \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n3+8+13+18 \\equiv 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:20).\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
